  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis reveals a strongly coherent signal centred on **eicosanoid biosynthesis and arachidonic acid metabolism**. The top-ranked pathway, **Eicosanoid synthesis (WikiPathways WP167)**, achieves an extraordinary 1,001-fold enrichment (FDR = 0.00) with five matched metabolites: arachidonic acid, leukotriene A4, 15-HETE, 5-HETE, and 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ2. This is further corroborated by the KEGG pathway **Arachidonic Acid Metabolism (map00590)**, confirming that the differentially abundant compounds are genuine members of the same biochemical module.\n\n**Key driver compounds.** Arachidonic acid (C00219) is the upstream metabolic hub for the entire cluster. Querying the KEGG reaction graph confirms it is directly connected to **leukotriene A4** (C00909; 2-hop path C00219 → C05356 → C00909, bidirectional) and to **15-HETE** (C04742; 2-hop path C00219 → C05966 → C04742, forward direction). Pathway membership was independently verified—arachidonic acid is annotated in the KEGG Arachidonic Acid Metabolism pathway, and all five eicosanoid compounds share this pathway with a co-occurrence plausibility of 1.0.\n\n**Biological significance.** Arachidonic acid is released from membrane phospholipids during inflammatory activation and serves as the substrate for three major enzymatic cascades: the cyclooxygenase pathway (producing prostaglandins such as 15-deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ2), the lipoxygenase pathway (producing 5-HETE and 15-HETE), and the 5-lipoxygenase branch that generates leukotriene A4. The coordinated elevation of these metabolites in the treatment group is consistent with robust phospholipase A2 activation and a substantive inflammatory or immune response. Meanwhile, the presence of dihydrothymine (a pyrimidine oxidation product) alongside hypochlorite and copper suggests concurrent oxidative DNA/base damage and neutrophil-driven halogenation activity. Histamine points to mast-cell degranulation, further implicating allergic-type or acute-phase inflammation.\n\n**Conclusion.** The data collectively indicate a strong perturbation of arachidonic acid metabolism and eicosanoid signalling, driven primarily by elevated arachidonic acid as the upstream substrate feeding into leukotriene and HETE production. The upstream regulatory event likely involves phospholipase activation or oxidative membrane stress.",
